('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000101', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'single_choice', 'A sequence begins 5, 8, 11, 14, … Which recursive definition generates this sequence?', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "A sequence begins 5, 8, 11, 14, … Which recursive definition generates this sequence?"}]}]}'::jsonb, 'Each term is 3 more than the previous term and the first term is 5, so a_1 = 5 and a_n = a_(n-1) + 3.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Each term is 3 more than the previous term and the first term is 5, so a_1 = 5 and a_n = a_(n-1) + 3."}]}]}'::jsonb, 2, 'medium', 'recursive formula', 'generating a sequence', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000102', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'single_choice', 'What is the explicit formula for the arithmetic sequence 3, 7, 11, 15, …?', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "What is the explicit formula for the arithmetic sequence 3, 7, 11, 15, …?"}]}]}'::jsonb, 'First term 3, common difference 4: a_n = 3 + (n − 1)4 = 4n − 1.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "First term 3, common difference 4: a_n = 3 + (n − 1)4 = 4n − 1."}]}]}'::jsonb, 2, 'medium', 'explicit formula', 'nth term of an arithmetic sequence', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000103', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'numeric', 'An arithmetic sequence has first term 7 and common difference 5. What is the 12th term?', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "An arithmetic sequence has first term 7 and common difference 5. What is the 12th term?"}]}]}'::jsonb, 'a_12 = 7 + (12 − 1) × 5 = 7 + 55 = 62.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "a_12 = 7 + (12 − 1) × 5 = 7 + 55 = 62."}]}]}'::jsonb, 2, 'medium', 'arithmetic sequence', 'nth term', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"numeric_answer": 62, "tolerance": 0}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000104', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'short_answer', 'What is the common ratio of the geometric sequence 2, 6, 18, 54, …? Enter a number.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "What is the common ratio of the geometric sequence 2, 6, 18, 54, …? Enter a number."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'Each term is multiplied by 3, so r = 3 (6 ÷ 2 = 18 ÷ 6 = 54 ÷ 18 = 3).', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Each term is multiplied by 3, so r = 3 (6 ÷ 2 = 18 ÷ 6 = 54 ÷ 18 = 3)."}]}]}'::jsonb, 2, 'medium', 'geometric sequence', 'common ratio', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000105', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'single_choice', 'The sequence 2, 6, 12, 20, … has first differences 4, 6, 8, … and constant second difference 2. Which formula gives the nth term?', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The sequence 2, 6, 12, 20, … has first differences 4, 6, 8, … and constant second difference 2. Which formula gives the nth term?"}]}]}'::jsonb, 'n^2 + n gives 2, 6, 12, 20 for n = 1, 2, 3, 4; the constant second difference 2 confirms a quadratic with leading coefficient 1.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "n^2 + n gives 2, 6, 12, 20 for n = 1, 2, 3, 4; the constant second difference 2 confirms a quadratic with leading coefficient 1."}]}]}'::jsonb, 3, 'medium', 'quadratic sequence', 'second differences', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000106', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'numeric', 'A quadratic sequence begins 5, 12, 23, 38, … What is the next term?', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "A quadratic sequence begins 5, 12, 23, 38, … What is the next term?"}]}]}'::jsonb, 'First differences are 7, 11, 15; the second difference is 4, so the next first difference is 19 and the next term is 38 + 19 = 57.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "First differences are 7, 11, 15; the second difference is 4, so the next first difference is 19 and the next term is 38 + 19 = 57."}]}]}'::jsonb, 3, 'medium', 'quadratic sequence', 'next term', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"numeric_answer": 57, "tolerance": 0}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000107', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'open_ended', 'Explain the difference between a recursive definition and an explicit formula for a sequence. Give one example of each for the same sequence and state one advantage of each form.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Explain the difference between a recursive definition and an explicit formula for a sequence."}]}, {"type": "bulletList", "content": [{"type": "listItem", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Give one example of each for the same sequence."}]}]}, {"type": "listItem", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "State one advantage of each form."}]}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Write in full sentences and use the mathematics editor for all expressions."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'Recursive: a₁ = 4, aₙ = aₙ₋₁ + 3 (needs previous term). Explicit: aₙ = 3n + 1 (works for any n directly). Advantages: recursion models step-by-step processes; explicit form finds a₁₀₀ without listing terms.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Recursive: a₁ = 4, aₙ = aₙ₋₁ + 3 (needs previous term). Explicit: aₙ = 3n + 1 (works for any n directly). Advantages: recursion models step-by-step processes; explicit form finds a₁₀₀ without listing terms."}]}]}'::jsonb, 5, 'medium', 'recursive formula', 'recursive and explicit definitions', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"response_tools": ["rich_text", "math", "table"], "response_requirement": "text", "response_guidance": "Define both forms, then use the same sequence (for example 4, 7, 10, 13, …) to illustrate each.", "marking_criteria": "1 mark: recursive definition uses the previous term(s) plus a starting value. 1 mark: explicit formula gives aₙ directly from n. 2 marks: correct matching examples for the same sequence (1 mark each). 1 mark: valid advantage of each form (for example recursion is easy to generate term by term; the explicit form finds a distant term quickly).", "myp_criterion": "C"}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000108', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'open_ended', 'Complete the table for a₁ = 4, aₙ = aₙ₋₁ + 3 and b₁ = 4, bₙ = 3bₙ₋₁ for n = 1 to 5. Then give a₅ and b₅, explain why the a-sequence is linear but the b-sequence is not, and identify the geometric sequence and its common ratio.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Two sequences are defined by a₁ = 4 with aₙ = aₙ₋₁ + 3, and b₁ = 4 with bₙ = 3bₙ₋₁."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Complete the table for n = 1 to 5, then answer the questions below."}]}, {"type": "table", "content": [{"type": "tableRow", "content": [{"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "n"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "1"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "2"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "3"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "4"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "5"}]}]}]}, {"type": "tableRow", "content": [{"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "a"}, {"type": "text", "text": "ₙ"}]}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}]}, {"type": "tableRow", "content": [{"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "b"}, {"type": "text", "text": "ₙ"}]}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(a) Give the value of a₅ and b₅. (b) Explain why the a-sequence is linear but the b-sequence is not. (c) Which sequence is geometric, and what is its common ratio?"}]}]}'::jsonb, 'a: 4, 7, 10, 13, 16 (a₅ = 16). b: 4, 12, 36, 108, 324 (b₅ = 324). a has constant difference 3, so linear. b has constant ratio 3, so geometric/exponential, not linear.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "a: 4, 7, 10, 13, 16 (a₅ = 16). b: 4, 12, 36, 108, 324 (b₅ = 324). a has constant difference 3, so linear. b has constant ratio 3, so geometric/exponential, not linear."}]}]}'::jsonb, 6, 'medium', 'linear sequence', 'tables, arithmetic and geometric sequences', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"response_tools": ["rich_text", "math", "table"], "response_guidance": "Insert a table for your values, then write your explanations underneath.", "marking_criteria": "3 marks: correct a-values 4, 7, 10, 13, 16. 2 marks: correct b-values 4, 12, 36, 108, 324. 1 mark: a-sequence has constant difference 3 (linear) while b-sequence has constant ratio 3 and grows multiplicatively (exponential), so b is geometric with r = 3. Award follow-through for arithmetic slips where the method is sound.", "myp_criterion": "A"}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000109', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'open_ended', 'For aₙ = n² − 2n, complete the table for n = 1 to 6, plot the points on a graph with n horizontal and aₙ vertical, describe the shape, show that the sequence is not arithmetic, and find a₇.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "A quadratic sequence is defined by aₙ = n² − 2n for n ≥ 1."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(a) Complete the table for n = 1 to 6."}]}, {"type": "table", "content": [{"type": "tableRow", "content": [{"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "n"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "1"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "2"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "3"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "4"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "5"}]}]}, {"type": "tableHeader", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "6"}]}]}]}, {"type": "tableRow", "content": [{"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "a"}, {"type": "text", "text": "ₙ"}]}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}, {"type": "tableCell", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": []}]}]}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(b) Plot the six points on the graph workspace, with n on the horizontal axis and aₙ on the vertical axis."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(c) Describe the shape of the points. (d) Show that the sequence is not arithmetic, and find the next term a₇."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'Values: −1, 0, 3, 8, 15, 24. First differences: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 (second difference 2). The points form a curve, so the sequence is quadratic, not arithmetic. a₇ = 49 − 14 = 35.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Values: −1, 0, 3, 8, 15, 24. First differences: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 (second difference 2). The points form a curve, so the sequence is quadratic, not arithmetic. a₇ = 49 − 14 = 35."}]}]}'::jsonb, 7, 'hard', 'quadratic sequence', 'graphing a quadratic sequence', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"response_tools": ["rich_text", "math", "table", "graph"], "response_graph": {"title": "aₙ = n² − 2n for n = 1 … 6", "instructions": "Plot the six points. Tap the point tool, then tap the grid at each coordinate pair.", "allow_functions": true, "starter": {"version": 1, "title": "aₙ = n² − 2n", "domain": {"xMin": 0, "xMax": 7, "yMin": -2, "yMax": 26, "gridStep": 1, "showGrid": true, "showAxes": true, "showCoordinates": true, "showLegend": true, "showIntersections": true, "equalScale": false}, "functions": [], "points": [], "segments": [], "annotations": []}}, "response_guidance": "Complete the table before plotting. Check each point against your table.", "response_requirement": "any", "marking_criteria": "2 marks: table values −1, 0, 3, 8, 15, 24. 3 marks: all six points plotted correctly with labelled axes (2 marks for 4–5 correct points). 1 mark: points lie on a curve (parabola-like), not a straight line. 1 mark: first differences 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 are not constant, so not arithmetic; a₇ = 35. Award follow-through for plotting errors that are consistent with the candidate''s table.", "myp_criterion": "C"}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000110', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'open_ended', 'An arithmetic sequence has first term 4 and common difference 5. Write the nth term, find the sum of the first 20 terms showing substitution, and explain why the sum formula works using pairing.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "An arithmetic sequence has first term 4 and common difference 5."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(a) Write the nth term."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(b) Find the sum of the first 20 terms, showing the substitution into the sum formula."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(c) Explain, using the idea of pairing terms, why the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series works."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'aₙ = 4 + (n − 1)5 = 5n − 1. S₂₀ = 20/2[2(4) + 19(5)] = 10(8 + 95) = 1030. Pairing: write S forwards and backwards; each of the n pairs adds to a + l, so 2S = n(a + l).', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "aₙ = 4 + (n − 1)5 = 5n − 1. S₂₀ = 20/2[2(4) + 19(5)] = 10(8 + 95) = 1030. Pairing: write S forwards and backwards; each of the n pairs adds to a + l, so 2S = n(a + l)."}]}]}'::jsonb, 6, 'medium', 'arithmetic series', 'sum of an arithmetic series', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"response_tools": ["rich_text", "math"], "response_guidance": "Write the formula before substituting. A correct final answer without method earns limited credit.", "marking_criteria": "1 mark: aₙ = 5n − 1. 1 mark: correct formula Sₙ = n/2[2a + (n − 1)d]. 1 mark: correct substitution with n = 20, a = 4, d = 5. 1 mark: S₂₀ = 1030. 2 marks: clear pairing explanation — writing the sum forwards and backwards, each pair sums to a + l, giving Sₙ = n/2(a + l).", "myp_criterion": "A"}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000111', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'numeric', 'Find the sum of the first 10 terms of the geometric series 3 + 6 + 12 + 24 + …', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Find the sum of the first 10 terms of the geometric series 3 + 6 + 12 + 24 + …"}]}]}'::jsonb, 'a = 3, r = 2, n = 10: S₁₀ = 3(2¹⁰ − 1)/(2 − 1) = 3(1023) = 3069.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "a = 3, r = 2, n = 10: S₁₀ = 3(2¹⁰ − 1)/(2 − 1) = 3(1023) = 3069."}]}]}'::jsonb, 3, 'medium', 'geometric series', 'finite geometric sum', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"numeric_answer": 3069, "tolerance": 0}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000112', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'open_ended', 'For the geometric series 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 + …, state a and r, find the sum to infinity, state the convergence condition and explain why the infinite sum is finite.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Consider the geometric series 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 + …"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(a) State the first term and the common ratio."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(b) Find the sum to infinity."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(c) State the condition on r for an infinite geometric series to converge, and explain what happens when the condition is not met."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(d) Explain why the sum to infinity is 16 even though the series has infinitely many terms."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'a = 8, r = 1/2. S∞ = 8/(1 − 1/2) = 16. Converges only when |r| < 1; the terms then approach 0 and partial sums approach a limit. If |r| ≥ 1, terms do not approach 0, so no finite sum exists.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "a = 8, r = 1/2. S∞ = 8/(1 − 1/2) = 16. Converges only when |r| < 1; the terms then approach 0 and partial sums approach a limit. If |r| ≥ 1, terms do not approach 0, so no finite sum exists."}]}]}'::jsonb, 6, 'medium', 'geometric series', 'sum to infinity and convergence', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"response_tools": ["rich_text", "math"], "response_guidance": "Use the formula S∞ = a/(1 − r), and justify the condition |r| < 1.", "marking_criteria": "1 mark: a = 8. 1 mark: r = 1/2. 1 mark: correct formula S∞ = a/(1 − r). 1 mark: S∞ = 16. 1 mark: condition |r| < 1. 1 mark: explanation that terms decrease geometrically and the partial sums approach 16; if |r| ≥ 1 the terms do not tend to zero and the series diverges (or has no finite sum).", "myp_criterion": "C"}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000113', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'open_ended', 'A salary starts at 1,200 and increases by 75 each month. Find a formula for month n, the month-13 salary, the total over 24 months, and the first month the salary exceeds 3,000.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "A company offers a starting monthly salary of 1,200 units of currency. The salary increases by 75 units at the end of every month, starting from month 2."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(a) Write a formula for the salary in month n."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(b) Find the salary in month 13."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(c) Find the total salary earned over the first 24 months."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(d) In which month does the monthly salary first exceed 3,000 units? Show the inequality you solve."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'aₙ = 1200 + 75(n − 1). Month 13: 1200 + 900 = 2100. S₂₄ = 24/2[2(1200) + 23(75)] = 12(2400 + 1725) = 49,500. 1200 + 75(n − 1) > 3000 → n − 1 > 24 → n > 25, so month 26.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "aₙ = 1200 + 75(n − 1). Month 13: 1200 + 900 = 2100. S₂₄ = 24/2[2(1200) + 23(75)] = 12(2400 + 1725) = 49,500. 1200 + 75(n − 1) > 3000 → n − 1 > 24 → n > 25, so month 26."}]}]}'::jsonb, 6, 'hard', 'arithmetic series', 'contextual modelling', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"response_tools": ["rich_text", "math", "table"], "response_guidance": "Model the salary as an arithmetic sequence. Show the inequality for part (d).", "marking_criteria": "1 mark: aₙ = 1200 + (n − 1)75. 1 mark: month 13 salary = 2,100. 1 mark: correct arithmetic-series setup for 24 months. 1 mark: total = 49,500. 1 mark: correct inequality 1200 + 75(n − 1) > 3000. 1 mark: month 26 (month 25 equals 3,000 exactly, so does not exceed it). Award follow-through for arithmetic slips in earlier parts.", "myp_criterion": "D"}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000114', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'open_ended', 'The triangular numbers are 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, … Write the next two terms, classify the sequence, show that Tₙ = n(n + 1)/2, find T₂₀, and explain the connection to arithmetic series.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The triangular numbers are 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, … Each term is the sum of the first n positive integers."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(a) Write the next two terms."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(b) State whether the sequence is arithmetic, geometric or neither, and justify your answer."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(c) Show that the nth triangular number is Tₙ = n(n + 1)/2."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(d) Hence find T₂₀."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(e) Explain how the triangular numbers connect to the sum of the arithmetic series 1 + 2 + 3 + … + n."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'Next terms 21, 28. Differences increase by 1, so neither arithmetic nor geometric. Pair 1 + n, 2 + (n − 1), …; there are n pairs each summing to n + 1, so 2Tₙ = n(n + 1) and Tₙ = n(n + 1)/2. T₂₀ = 20 × 21/2 = 210. Tₙ is exactly Sₙ for the arithmetic series 1 + 2 + … + n (a = 1, d = 1).', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Next terms 21, 28. Differences increase by 1, so neither arithmetic nor geometric. Pair 1 + n, 2 + (n − 1), …; there are n pairs each summing to n + 1, so 2Tₙ = n(n + 1) and Tₙ = n(n + 1)/2. T₂₀ = 20 × 21/2 = 210. Tₙ is exactly Sₙ for the arithmetic series 1 + 2 + … + n (a = 1, d = 1)."}]}]}'::jsonb, 7, 'hard', 'arithmetic series', 'triangular numbers and proof', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"response_tools": ["rich_text", "math"], "response_guidance": "For part (c), a written argument is required — a single formula earns no marks without justification.", "marking_criteria": "1 mark: next terms 21 and 28. 1 mark: neither arithmetic nor geometric, with a valid reason (differences 2, 3, 4, 5 are not constant; ratios are not constant). 3 marks: derivation of Tₙ = n(n + 1)/2, for example pairing terms of 1 + 2 + … + n with n + (n − 1) + … + 1 to obtain n(n + 1) and halving (2 marks for a partially complete pairing argument). 1 mark: T₂₀ = 210. 1 mark: identifies Tₙ as the sum of the arithmetic series with a = 1 and d = 1.", "myp_criterion": "A"}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000115', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'multiple_select', 'Select every sequence that is geometric.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Select every sequence that is geometric."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'Geometric sequences have a constant ratio: 2, 6, 18, 54 (r = 3); 1, 2, 4, 8 (r = 2); 81, 27, 9, 3 (r = 1/3); 1, −2, 4, −8 (r = −2). 3, 6, 9, 12 has a constant difference, not a constant ratio.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Geometric sequences have a constant ratio: 2, 6, 18, 54 (r = 3); 1, 2, 4, 8 (r = 2); 81, 27, 9, 3 (r = 1/3); 1, −2, 4, −8 (r = −2). 3, 6, 9, 12 has a constant difference, not a constant ratio."}]}]}'::jsonb, 3, 'medium', 'geometric sequence', 'identifying geometric sequences', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000116', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'open_ended', 'A student claims 2, 5, 8, 11, … is geometric with common ratio 3. Identify the error, classify the sequence correctly, and explain common difference versus common ratio.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "A student writes: “The sequence 2, 5, 8, 11, … is geometric because you add 3 each time. The common ratio is 3.”"}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(a) Identify the error in the student''s reasoning."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(b) Classify the sequence correctly, giving the common difference."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(c) Explain the difference between a common difference and a common ratio, using this sequence and a geometric example."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'Adding 3 creates a common difference, so the sequence is arithmetic with d = 3, not geometric. The ratios 5/2 and 8/5 are not equal. A geometric example is 2, 6, 18, 54 with constant ratio 3.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Adding 3 creates a common difference, so the sequence is arithmetic with d = 3, not geometric. The ratios 5/2 and 8/5 are not equal. A geometric example is 2, 6, 18, 54 with constant ratio 3."}]}]}'::jsonb, 5, 'medium', 'arithmetic sequence', 'error analysis', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"response_tools": ["rich_text", "math"], "response_guidance": "Quote the student''s words where useful, then correct them precisely.", "marking_criteria": "1 mark: adding a constant is a common difference, not a ratio. 1 mark: the sequence is arithmetic with d = 3. 1 mark: ratio test shows 5/2 = 2.5, 8/5 = 1.6, so there is no common ratio. 2 marks: clear contrast — common difference compares consecutive terms by subtraction and produces linear growth; common ratio compares consecutive terms by division and produces geometric growth, e.g. 2, 6, 18, 54 with r = 3.", "myp_criterion": "C"}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual'),
('a5000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000117', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001', 'open_ended', 'For aₙ = 2n + 1 and bₙ = 2ⁿ⁻¹, write the first five terms, plot both sets of points, and compare the growth of the two graphs.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "An arithmetic sequence has aₙ = 2n + 1. A geometric sequence has bₙ = 2ⁿ⁻¹."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(a) Write the first five terms of each sequence."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(b) Plot both sets of points on the same axes in the graph workspace, for n = 1 to 5."}]}, {"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "(c) Describe how the two graphs differ, and explain why the geometric sequence eventually grows much faster."}]}]}'::jsonb, 'Arithmetic: 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 (collinear, slope 2). Geometric: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 (doubling, increasingly steep). Linear growth adds a constant; exponential growth multiplies by a constant, so it eventually dominates.', '{"type": "doc", "content": [{"type": "paragraph", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Arithmetic: 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 (collinear, slope 2). Geometric: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 (doubling, increasingly steep). Linear growth adds a constant; exponential growth multiplies by a constant, so it eventually dominates."}]}]}'::jsonb, 5, 'medium', 'geometric sequence', 'comparing arithmetic and geometric graphs', array['MYP 5','sequences and series','formative'], 'published', '{"response_tools": ["rich_text", "math", "graph"], "response_graph": {"title": "Arithmetic versus geometric growth", "instructions": "Plot both sets of points. Label which set is arithmetic and which is geometric.", "allow_functions": true, "starter": {"version": 1, "title": "Arithmetic versus geometric growth", "domain": {"xMin": 0, "xMax": 6, "yMin": 0, "yMax": 20, "gridStep": 1, "showGrid": true, "showAxes": true, "showCoordinates": true, "showLegend": true, "showIntersections": true, "equalScale": false}, "functions": [], "points": [], "segments": [], "annotations": []}}, "response_guidance": "Use a different label or colour for each sequence so the two sets of points can be compared.", "marking_criteria": "1 mark: arithmetic terms 3, 5, 7, 9, 11. 1 mark: geometric terms 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. 2 marks: both sets plotted accurately (1 mark if one set is correct or points are mislabelled). 1 mark: comparison — arithmetic points lie on a straight line with constant difference 2; geometric points rise increasingly steeply because each term is multiplied by 2, so growth is multiplicative and overtakes linear growth.", "myp_criterion": "D"}'::jsonb, '3c3ed712-f409-4aa1-a645-816418e22ec4', 'MYP', 'MYP 5 Mathematics', '0a400000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030', 'approved', 'manual')
on conflict (id) do nothing;

